## What happened

Apple's security libraries never supported DSA, so .NET on macOS relied on OpenSSL via the system's `libcrypto`. Microsoft has been phasing out built-in DSA support — starting with obsoletion warnings (`SYSLIB0030`) in .NET 7 and progressively removing platform implementations. In .NET 11, `DSA.Create()` now throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS (it was already unsupported on iOS/tvOS/MacCatalyst earlier).

## How to fix it

You have a few options, depending on your constraints:

### 1. Use a cross-platform OpenSSL binding (recommended for legacy verification)

Reference the [`System.Security.Cryptography.OpenSsl`](https://www.nuget.org/packages/System.Security.Cryptography.OpenSsl) package or use OpenSSL via interop. On macOS, install OpenSSL with Homebrew and load it explicitly:

```csharp
// Use DSAOpenSsl directly (requires OpenSSL installed on the host)
using System.Security.Cryptography;

using var dsa = new DSAOpenSsl(); // works on Linux & macOS with OpenSSL
dsa.ImportParameters(legacyParams);
bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA1);
```

### 2. Migrate away from DSA entirely (best long-term)

DSA is deprecated everywhere. If you only need to **verify** existing signatures (not generate new ones), consider a one-time re-signing migration to ECDSA or Ed25519, then drop DSA.

### 3. Use BouncyCastle as a managed fallback

```csharp
// No native dependency required
var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA");
signer.Init(false, publicKey);
signer.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
bool valid = signer.VerifySignature(signature);
```

---

**TL;DR:** .NET 11 removed the macOS DSA implementation. Use `DSAOpenSsl` with a Homebrew OpenSSL, a managed library like BouncyCastle, or migrate to a modern algorithm.
